<p>All or Nothing put the difficult 2021/22 season on camera. Ownership backed the rebuild with more than £900m of signings by August 2025. <a href="#A6">[A6, </a><a href="#F10">F10]</a></p>
<details>
<summary>The rebuild in detail</summary>
<p>All or Nothing: Arsenal is an eight-part Amazon Prime Video series first released on 4 August 2022, following Arteta and the squad through 2021/22 with access to Emirates Stadium and London Colney. BBC Sport reported in August 2025 that spending on signings under Arteta was passing £900m, with more than £80m in every full season: £143m in 2021/22, £159m in 2022/23 and £199.3m in 2023/24, the fourth-highest outlay in the league since 2020/21. Arsenal writer James McNicholas summed up the culture reset on Arsecast Extra episode 508, 23 January 2023, as transcribed by listeners: Wenger built the Emirates and Arteta turned the lights on. <a href="#A6">[A6, </a><a href="#F10">F10, </a><a href="#F11">F11]</a></p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>The thesis and its causal limit</summary>
<p>The author&#x27;s thesis is that success on the pitch brought about the commercial deal success. The timing supports it as the strongest interpretation: commercial revenue grew 55.5% across FY2023-25 as finishes improved, and the 2026 Emirates renewal with its reported uplift followed the title. The limit stays visible: transfer investment, market-wide commercial growth and retail execution moved in the same period, so the contribution of winning cannot be isolated from public data. <a href="#A1">[A1, </a><a href="#A2">A2, </a><a href="#F9">F9, </a><a href="#F10">F10, </a><a href="#P1">P1, </a><a href="#P2">P2]</a></p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="partners">
<p class="kick">PARTNERSHIP PORTFOLIO · 04</p>
<h2>Eight verified announcements, four value layers.</h2>
<div class="stepper">
<div class="tl"><p class="when">18 Jul 2025</p><p class="what"><strong>Airwallex</strong> finance software partner, Asia tour and digital visibility. Undisclosed value. <a href="#P10">[P10, </a><a href="#P11">P11]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">22 Jul 2025</p><p class="what"><strong>Guinness</strong> global club partner, stadium and supporter-club rituals. Undisclosed. <a href="#P9">[P9]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">20 Aug 2025</p><p class="what"><strong>Coca-Cola</strong> global soft-drinks partner for two years, men&#x27;s and women&#x27;s matchdays. Undisclosed. <a href="#P8">[P8]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">12 Dec 2025</p><p class="what"><strong>Deel</strong> HR platform partner, multi-year. Undisclosed. <a href="#P3">[P3]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">9 Apr 2026</p><p class="what"><strong>WhatsApp + Facebook</strong> global partnership, digital experiences and stadium activations. Undisclosed. <a href="#P5">[P5]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">14 May 2026</p><p class="what"><strong>Deel</strong> left sleeve from 2026/27 across home, away and third shirts. Undisclosed. <a href="#P4">[P4]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">6 Aug 2026</p><p class="what"><strong>Emirates</strong> renewed through 2033: front of shirt, training kit and stadium naming rights. SportsPro reports up to £70m per season against a previous figure understood as up to £50m. <a href="#P1">[P1, </a><a href="#P2">P2]</a></p></div>
<div class="tl"><p class="when">29 Oct 2026</p><p class="what"><strong>Oakley Meta</strong> official AI glasses partner, We Go Again campaign film. Undisclosed. <a href="#P6">[P6, </a><a href="#P7">P7]</a></p></div>
