<section id="measure">
<p class="kick">NIELSEN MEASUREMENT LENS · 07</p>
<h2>Measure exposure, business value and social value separately.</h2>
<div class="cards">
<article><h3>Exposure</h3><p>Verified cross-platform media exposure by partner and category.</p></article>
<article><h3>Business outcomes</h3><p>Attributable qualified actions within a defined attribution window.</p></article>
<article><h3>Social outcomes</h3><p>Participation and paid creative opportunities, independently assessed.</p></article>
</div>
<details>
<summary>The measurement lens, mapped to Nielsen Sports services</summary>
<p>Nielsen Sports covers sponsorship media valuation, fan insights and research and consulting. This case separates exposure, brand outcomes, business outcomes and social outcomes rather than replicating any proprietary valuation. For the club: monitor rights revenue, activation delivery, servicing cost and renewal intent by category. For sponsors: verified cross-platform exposure, then awareness and consideration, then attributable qualified actions with a defined attribution window. For communities: participation, repeat attendance, paid creative opportunities and independently assessed outcomes. A campaign&#x27;s media value is not a measure of reduced youth violence. <a href="#N1">[N1-N3]</a></p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Research design and evidence grading</summary>
<p>Question: how has Arsenal strengthened its commercial model, and which outcomes are established rather than assumed? Scope: community and identity examples from 2022 onward, a like-for-like FY2023-25 financial series and partnership announcements through 24 November 2026. Method: club announcements cross-checked against partner releases, annual accounts, league and UEFA publications and independent reporting. Rights, dates, stated activation, term and disclosed value go into a deal register. Observations, issuer claims, calculations and analyst interpretations stay separate. <a href="#A1">[A1-A3]</a></p>
<p>Evidence grading: an audited financial line carries high confidence for that period. An official announcement proves rights and date, not effectiveness. A partner product release is issuer evidence. A media contract value is a reported estimate. Community and interview evidence is qualitative, not a representative fan survey.</p>
<p>Bias controls: the author is an Arsenal supporter, so the analysis includes the losses, the European dependency, cost growth, fan-trust risks, alternative explanations and evidence gaps. No interviews, fan survey, social listening dataset, proprietary Nielsen data or paid source was used. No causal or econometric model is claimed.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Business concepts applied</summary>
<p>Value chain: football and event delivery create the experience, media distribute it, rights and retail monetize it, and software partners such as Airwallex and Deel connect support activities to the sponsorship proposition. The public evidence shows the links, not their cost advantage. <a href="#B1">[B1, </a><a href="#P3">P3, </a><a href="#P4">P4, </a><a href="#P10">P10, </a><a href="#P11">P11]</a></p>
<p>Segmentation and positioning: the evidence supports different propositions for local participants, global digital fans, fashion buyers and business-software decision makers. These are proposed analytical segments, not quantified audience groups. <a href="#B2">[B2, </a><a href="#C5">C5, </a><a href="#P3">P3-P5]</a></p>
<p>Bargaining power: local identity, global reach and stronger sporting performance may differentiate the rights bundle. The Emirates term and reported uplift are consistent with that, but no bid history is public and a full Five Forces assessment is not claimed. <a href="#P1">[P1, </a><a href="#P2">P2, </a><a href="#F4">F4, </a><a href="#F8">F8]</a></p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Competition economics</summary>
<p>Champions League distributions drove the FY2024 broadcast increase. FY2025 accounts disclose £99.9m in men&#x27;s Champions League distributions versus £80.4m a year earlier, components already inside broadcast revenue. UEFA&#x27;s report lists the men&#x27;s distribution separately as €116.998m. Currencies and reporting bases are preserved, not added together. UEFA&#x27;s 2024/25 men&#x27;s club competitions generated €4.4bn and distributed €3.4bn to clubs, ecosystem totals rather than Arsenal receipts. Arsenal Women won the 2024/25 Champions League, and several announcements include women&#x27;s assets, but no monetary share of bundled sponsorship is assigned without disclosure. <a href="#A1">[A1, </a><a href="#A2">A2, </a><a href="#A4">A4, </a><a href="#F1">F1, </a><a href="#F2">F2, </a><a href="#F3">F3]</a></p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="roadmap">
<p class="kick">RECOMMENDATIONS · 08</p>
<h2>Protect the identity. Prove the commercial outcome.</h2>
<div class="cards">
<article><h3>1 · Ledger</h3><p>Reconcile recognized rights revenue to the accounts before quoting any portfolio value.</p></article>
<article><h3>2 · Measurement plans</h3><p>Separate exposure, brand, business and operational outcomes per partner. No ROI claim without cost and attribution assumptions.</p></article>
<article><h3>3 · Community track</h3><p>Independent objectives for No More Red and local-business support. Youth programmes stay separate from alcohol marketing.</p></article>
<article><h3>4 · Scenarios</h3><p>Model European qualification and cost growth quarterly, without assuming sponsorship offsets lost broadcast revenue.</p></article>
</div>
<details>
<summary>Roadmap in full</summary>
<p>Priority 1, first review cycle: create a rights and economics ledger reconciling recognized revenue to accounts. No headline portfolio value until overlapping bundles and confidential terms are resolved.</p>
<p>Priority 2, next planning cycle: agree partner-specific measurement plans separating exposure, brand, business and operational outcomes. No ROI claim without cost, outcomes, comparison and attribution assumptions.</p>
<p>Priority 3, parallel community track: independent objectives for No More Red and local-business support, tracking paid opportunities and durable participation, with youth programmes kept separate from alcohol marketing.</p>
<p>Priority 4, quarterly scenario review: model competition risk and cost growth across European qualification and fixture scenarios without assuming sponsorship automatically offsets lost broadcast revenue.</p>
<p>These are analyst recommendations, not statements of Arsenal&#x27;s current internal process.</p>
</details>
</section>

